\"A\"}, entry B {id:\"dup\", order:2, text:\"B\"}. Sorted → [A(1), B(2)], keep last → B. Assert system has 1 entry with text \"B\". ✓ matches \"后者覆盖\" (B comes later in order). Actually, hmm, \"后者\" here = the one with higher order (later in sorted array). Let me also test with same order to pin \"later provider wins\" via stable sort? That might be fragile. I'll keep orders different and assert B wins. That's clean and deterministic.\n\nActually wait, let me reconsider: with my `filter` dedup \"keep last in sorted array\", for [A(1),B(2)] → keep B (last). ✓. Good.\n\nLet me also double check the hard standard test: `collectInjections([guardProvider], ctx).system[0].text === INJECTION_GUARD`. guardProvider returns one entry channel \"system\". collectInjections → system=[entry]. system[0].text = INJECTION_GUARD. ✓. But NOTE: guardProvider respects `injectionGuardEnabled()`. In the test env, `AGENT_INJECTION_GUARD` is presumably unset → enabled. But to be robust, I'll make the test explicitly delete the env var or set it to on. Actually, I'll make `guardProvider` NOT depend on the env switch directly — instead, let me reconsider.\n\nHmm, decision: where does `injectionGuardEnabled()` get checked? \n\nOption A: guardProvider checks it (returns undefined when off).\nOption B: guardProvider always returns the entry; lifecycle's section render checks `injectionGuardEnabled()` before calling collectInjections.\n\nFor \"逐字节一致\": when off, section must render undefined. \n- Option A: guardProvider returns undefined → collectInjections → system=[] → `.system[0]?.text` = undefined. ✓.\n- Option B: section render does `if (!injectionGuardEnabled()) return undefined;` then collectInjections([guardProvider]). ✓.\n\nFor the hard-standard test `collectInjections([guardProvider], ctx).system[0].text === INJECTION_GUARD`: \n- Option A: requires guard enabled in test env. If some other test set AGENT_INJECTION_GUARD=false and didn't restore, could be flaky. injection-guard.test.ts restores it in finally. But test ordering... node:test runs files, each file is a separate process? Actually with `tsx --test file1 file2 ...`, all files run in the same process? Or separate? Node's test runner runs each file in a separate process by default (isolation). Actually `node --test` spawns a separate process per test file. So env changes in one file don't affect another. So it's safe. But to be safe I'll have my test explicitly set the env on.\n\n- Option B: guardProvider always returns the entry → hard-standard test passes regardless of env. Simpler for the test.\n\nWhich is cleaner architecturally? The enable switch is a property of the INJECTION (whether this injection is active). Putting it in the provider (Option A) makes the framework \"know\" about active/inactive injections — more cohesive (the framework is the single source of truth for what's injected). Putting it in lifecycle (Option B) means lifecycle still has special-cased guard logic, which is less \"takeover\". \n\nI prefer Option A: guardProvider encapsulates the switch. The framework then fully owns the guard. But then the hard-standard test needs guard enabled. Let me make guardProvider's enable check injectable via ctx or a param to avoid env dependence in the pure function... \n\nActually, cleanest: `guardProvider` reads `injectionGuardEnabled()` (env). The hard-standard test will `delete process.env.AGENT_INJECTION_GUARD` (or set to \"on\") at the start. Robust. Let me do that. Actually, even simpler: I'll design `guardProvider` to accept the enabled state from ctx optionally. Hmm, over-engineering.\n\nLet me reconsider: to make `collectInjections([guardProvider], ctx)` ALWAYS yield the guard (for the hard-standard assertion, which should be unconditional per the task), and still respect the switch for the ACTUAL injection, I could:\n- `guardProvider` always returns the entry (pure, no env read).\n- The ENABLE switch is applied in lifecycle's `render`: `render: (input) => injectionGuardEnabled() ? collectInjections([guardProvider], ctx).system[0].text : undefined`.\n\nWait, but then when disabled, the section renders undefined (✓ byte-identical), and the framework's guardProvider always emits. The audit endpoint — should it reflect the switch? If disabled, the guard isn't injected, so arguably audit should show 0 rows or show it as inactive. Hmm. But the audit is about the framework's injections. Let me think: if `AGENT_INJECTION_GUARD=false`, the guard is NOT injected. The audit should reflect reality → 0 rows (or show disabled). \n\nHmm, this is getting complicated. Let me step back and choose the design that (a) satisfies the hard standard unconditionally, (b) preserves byte-identical injection including the off-switch, (c) is clean.\n\n**Cleanest design:**\n- `guardProvider: InjectionProvider` = `(ctx) => injectionGuardEnabled() ? { id:\"injection_guard\", text: INJECTION_GUARD, order: GUARD_ORDER, scope:\"session\", channel:\"system\" } : undefined`. Reads env internally.\n- The hard-standard test sets `AGENT_INJECTION_GUARD` to on (or deletes) — I'll ensure it. Actually, let me make the test NOT rely on env by having guardProvider's enable be overridable... no.\n\nHmm, wait. Actually, re-reading the hard standard: \"单测直接断言 collectInjections([guardProvider], ctx).system[0].text === INJECTION_GUARD\". This is the acceptance test. The task author expects this to pass. If guardProvider reads env and the test doesn't set env, it relies on default (enabled). That's fine — default is enabled. I'll write the test to delete the env var at the start to be deterministic. \n\nBut actually, there's a cleaner idea: make `guardProvider` NOT read env (always emit), and handle the switch OUTSIDE the framework in lifecycle. Then:\n- The framework (collectInjections, guardProvider) is pure and the hard-standard test is unconditional and robust.\n- lifecycle: `render: (input) => injectionGuardEnabled() ? collectInjections([guardProvider], ctx).system[0]?.text : undefined`. \n   - When on: renders INJECTION_GUARD (byte-identical). ✓\n   - When off: renders undefined. ✓\n- Audit endpoint: should it respect the switch? The audit endpoint returns the framework's injection inventory. If I want it to reflect \"what's actually injected\", I'd gate it on the switch too. But the audit switch is `AGENT_INJECTION_AUDIT`, separate from `AGENT_INJECTION_GUARD`. Hmm. I think the audit should show the guard entry as long as the audit switch is on — it's an inventory of framework-managed injections, not \"currently active\". Actually, let me make the audit reflect the collected entries (which includes the guard unconditionally since guardProvider always emits). That's simplest.\n\nBut then \"接管\" — with Option B, lifecycle still calls `injectionGuardEnabled()` directly (not fully \"owned\" by framework). Is that a problem? The task's \"接管\" means the guard's CONTENT and POSITION flow through the framework. With Option B, the content (text) comes from `collectInjections([guardProvider])` — yes, through the framework. The switch is still checked in lifecycle but that's a conditional, not the content. I think that's acceptable. But a purist might say the framework should own the enable too.\n\nAlternatively Option A (guardProvider reads env) makes the framework fully own the guard, and lifecycle becomes `render: (input) => collectInjections([guardProvider], ctx).system[0]?.text` — cleaner! The switch is encapsulated in the provider. When off, guardProvider returns undefined → collectInjections → system empty → `.system[0]?.text` = undefined → section omitted. ✓ Byte-identical. And hard-standard test needs env on (default). \n\nI'll go with **Option A** (framework fully owns the guard), and write the hard-standard test to explicitly ensure the env is enabled. This is the cleaner \"takeover\". And the audit (using the same guardProvider) naturally reflects the switch: when guard is off, audit returns 0 rows. That's actually MORE correct (audit reflects reality). \n\nWait, but then the audit's \"只接管了一个\" test — if guard is off in that test's env, audit returns 0 rows, failing \"1 row\". So my test must ensure guard is on. I'll set env in tests. Fine.\n\nHmm, but actually, there's a subtle concern: the hard-standard assertion `collectInjections([guardProvider], ctx).system[0].text` — if guardProvider returns undefined (guard off), `system` is empty, `system[0]` is undefined, `.text` throws. So the test MUST have guard on. I'll ensure that. Since default is on, and I'll explicitly set it, fine.\n\nOK, let me also reconsider the `order` value for the guard. Let me look at whether other sections might be relevant. Actually, let me set `order = 30` or something representing position. But since only one provider, any number works. I'll define `GUARD_ORDER = 3` matching its section index, with a comment. Actually, to be meaningful, let me use the section index (3). But that couples to lifecycle. Alternatively, just `order: 0`. Hmm. Let me use a named constant `INJECTION_GUARD_ORDER = 30` (leaving room for future providers). Eh. I'll just use `order: 0` with a comment \"批 A 只有这一个 provider，order 仅用于框架内排序\". Actually, let me use a small dedicated constant. I'll set `order: 10`. Doesn't matter. Let me just pick `0`. Keep it simple. Actually, for the \"排序稳定\" test I'll use DIFFERENT providers with various orders, not the guard. So guard's order is irrelevant to tests. I'll set `order: 0`.\n\nHmm, wait, but \"逐字节一致\" about POSITION — the reviewer might check that the guard section is still at the same index in the `sections` array. I'll keep it at index 3 (unchanged). ✓. The `order` field is internal to the framework and doesn't affect the sections array position. Good.\n\nNow let me also think about `scope`. The guard is a `session` scope? The section renders every session (it's in the extension sections, rendered per conversation). `scope: \"session\"` seems right (it's session-level, present for the whole session). Or \"once\"/\"turn\"? The guard is injected as a section every time durable renders sections — which is once per session (sections are frozen per session, appended when values change). Actually durable sections are rendered and stored as `pi.system` entries; they persist for the session. So `scope: \"session\"` is appropriate. I'll use \"session\".\n\nNow the `InjectionContext`. Let me define minimal:\n```\nexport interface InjectionContext {\n  /** 会话 id（durable 内部或对外 id 皆可；框架只用其作上下文，不下发） */\n  conversationId: number | string;\n}\n```\nFor lifecycle render, `input.conversationId` — I'll pass `Number(input.conversationId)` or as-is. Let me check the type. In lifecycle line 500: `partnersStore().partnerIdOfSession(Number(input.conversationId))`. So `input.conversationId` is likely a string or number; `Number()` is applied. I'll pass `input.conversationId` directly (typed as number|string). Fine.\n\nFor the audit endpoint, ctx = `{ conversationId: c.req.param(\"conversationId\") }` (a string). Fine.\n\nNow, let me check the http probe to make sure my endpoint won't break it.\n\nLet me read scripts/http-probe.ts.\n\nAlso I need to check the full `test` script to see if auxiliary.test.ts is included, and decide where to put new tests. Let me also check if there's a glob-based test discovery or an explicit list. The `test` script has `tsx --test tsx --test <long list>`. Wait, it says `tsx --test tsx --test ...` — there's a doubled `tsx --test tsx --test`? That looks like a typo but whatever. Let me read the full line.\n\nLet me grep the full test script from package.json (it might be one very long line).",
