      "reasoning": "Good. Now let me check the auth middleware behavior quickly, to ensure my endpoint is reachable (though the probe doesn't hit it). Actually, since the http probe doesn't hit my endpoint and I'm not adding a probe for it (the task doesn't require an http probe test for it, just that `pnpm ... http` passes), the auth doesn't matter much. But let me verify the endpoint returns properly. Actually, wait — the task says the http probe must pass. Adding a new endpoint that's never hit can't break it. But I want to make sure adding the import doesn't break the server startup. Since injection.ts is pure, it's fine.\n\nBut hold on — I should consider whether to ADD a probe check for the new endpoint. The task says \"审计端点参考 index.ts 里其它 ... 的写法\" and requires `pnpm --filter @lifediary/server http` to pass. It doesn't explicitly require adding a probe assertion for the endpoint. But to be thorough and to actually VERIFY the endpoint works, I could add a small check to the http-probe.ts. Hmm, but that modifies the probe which is a \"real service + real HTTP\" test. Adding a check would be good verification. However, the task says \"每完成一节跑一次 typecheck + test\" and the final verification includes `http`. It doesn't say to modify the http probe. \n\nLet me think: should I add an http probe assertion for `/injections`? Pros: actually verifies the endpoint end-to-end. Cons: modifies a large shared probe file (more surface). The task's \"审计端点\" is a new feature; verifying it via the http probe would be good practice. But the铁律 says \"不碰\" a list of files — http-probe.ts is NOT in that list. So I'm allowed to touch it. \n\nHmm, but adding to the probe means I need to be careful about where (which server instance is running, what env). The audit endpoint is simple (no LLM config needed, no conversation resolution). I could add a check early (e.g., in section 4 area) that GETs `/api/v1/agent/chat/<someId>/injections` and verifies the shape. Let me consider.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the endpoint design re: conversation validation. If I DON'T call openChat, the endpoint works regardless of conversation existence. That's simplest and means any conversationId works. Let me go with that — it's a diagnostic/audit endpoint listing framework-managed injections, not conversation data. I'll note this as a known boundary.\n\nActually wait — but is it weird that the path has `:conversationId` but the response is conversation-independent? The task explicitly specifies that path. I'll keep the path but treat conversationId as ctx only. The audit reflects the framework's injections (which for the guard are global/static). I'll document this boundary. \n\nHmm, but a reviewer might expect the audit to at least 404 on unknown conversation (like `/messages`, `/context`). Let me reconsider. Actually, for consistency with other `/chat/:conversationId/*` GET endpoints, they resolve the chat. But those return conversation data. The injections audit returns framework inventory. I think it's cleaner to NOT require a valid conversation (it's a global diagnostic). But to avoid surprise, maybe I should validate. Let me think about the probe: if I add a check hitting `/injections` with `externalId` (a valid conversation), both designs work. \n\nLet me make a decision: I'll make the endpoint NOT depend on openChat/LLM config (pure framework read) — this is more robust (works in mock/faux, no DB needed, no 404 for unknown id). I'll clearly document that it's a framework-level inventory and conversationId is only used as context. This also makes the http probe check trivial (works with any id, any env). \n\nHmm, but actually, wait. Let me reconsider — is there a risk that returning injection metadata for ANY conversationId (even a non-existent one) is a \"data boundary\" issue? The audit returns only `{id, source, scope, length}` — no conversation content, no PII. It's static framework config. So no privacy concern. Good.\n\nOK let me also reconsider: should the audit respect `AGENT_INJECTION_GUARD` (the guard's own switch)? With Option A (guardProvider checks injectionGuardEnabled), the audit naturally returns 0 rows when the guard is off. That's fine — audit reflects active injections. But it also means if `AGENT_INJECTION_GUARD=false`, audit returns `[]`. Is that desired? Probably fine. But for \"只接管了一个\" test, I need guard on. I'll set env in tests.\n\nHmm, actually, let me reconsider Option A vs the hard-standard test once more. The hard-standard assertion is `collectInjections([guardProvider], ctx).system[0].text === INJECTION_GUARD`. If guardProvider reads env and the test process has `AGENT_INJECTION_GUARD` unset → enabled → works. But to be bulletproof, I'll write the test to explicitly `delete process.env.AGENT_INJECTION_GUARD` at the start (and restore). Actually simpler: I'll make guardProvider's enable check overridable via a parameter with a default. Hmm.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: maybe the cleanest is for `guardProvider` to NOT read env at all (pure), always emit the guard entry. Then:\n- Hard-standard test: unconditional pass. ✓\n- lifecycle: `render: (input) => injectionGuardEnabled() ? collectInjections([guardProvider], ctx).system[0]?.text : undefined`.\n   - Hmm, but this reintroduces the switch check in lifecycle. \n- Or lifecycle: `render: (input) => collectInjections(activeProviders(ctx), ctx).system[0]?.text` where `activeProviders` filters by switch. \n\nWait, actually there's an even cleaner idea: put the enable switch INTO the provider but make it a SEPARATE concern. Define `guardProvider` to always emit, and define the registry to include a `gate` (enabled predicate). Then `collectInjections` only collects from providers whose gate passes... no, that complicates collectInjections signature.\n\nLet me just go with the SIMPLE, robust design:\n\n**guardProvider** reads `injectionGuardEnabled()` internally (module-level, env-based), returns the entry or undefined. This keeps ALL guard logic (content + switch) inside `injection.ts` — a true \"takeover\". lifecycle `render` becomes `collectInjections([guardProvider], ctx).system[0]?.text` — no special-casing. \n\nFor the hard-standard + \"只接管了一个\" tests, I'll explicitly set `process.env.AGENT_INJECTION_GUARD = \"on\"` (or delete) at the top of the test file's relevant tests. Actually, since node:test runs each FILE in its own process, and the default is enabled, I can just rely on default. But to be safe against a developer running with the env set, I'll set it in a `beforeEach`-like manner. Actually node:test has `before`/`after` hooks. I'll just set it in each test via a helper that saves/restores. Simpler: at the top of the test file, do `delete process.env.AGENT_INJECTION_GUARD;` — but that's a module-level side effect that runs at import; if another test file in the SAME process set it, this could interfere. Since files are separate processes, it's fine. Actually, to be clean, I'll use a per-test save/restore.\n\nHmm, let me reconsider — is it definitely true that `tsx --test file1 file2 ...` runs each file in a separate process? Node's test runner (`node --test`) by default runs test files in separate child processes (isolation: 'process'). Yes, each file is isolated. So env changes don't leak. Good.\n\nBut within my injection.test.ts file, if I set `AGENT_INJECTION_GUARD=false` in one test and don't restore, a later test could fail. I'll save/restore in each test that touches it. Or I'll just have most tests not touch it (rely on default on) and only the \"switch off\" test (if I add one) save/restore. Actually I don't need to test the guard switch off (that's injection-guard.test.ts's job). I'll just ensure guard is on in the hard-standard test.\n\nOK, decision finalized. Let me also reconsider whether to make `injectionAuditEnabled` gate at request-time. Yes.\n\nNow, let me also reconsider the \"source\" field value once more. I'll set it to `\"injection_guard\"`. Actually, to make `source