        "text": "§一 is done. Mark task 1 complete and start task 2.\n\nNow §二. Let me create injection.ts. Let me write the file.\n\nWait, before writing, let me reconsider the `source` design once more, and finalize. I'll go with a registry `INJECTION_REGISTRY` with `{ source, provider }`. And `auditInjections` calls `collectInjections([provider], ctx)` per registry entry.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: for the hard-standard \"只接管了一个\" test, I'll assert `INJECTION_REGISTRY.length === 1`. And also that `auditInjections(ctx)` returns exactly 1 row when guard is on, with id `injection_guard`.\n\nLet me also reconsider: should `collectInjections` accept `readonly InjectionProvider[]`? The hard-standard calls `collectInjections([guardProvider], ctx)`. Array literal → assignable to `readonly InjectionProvider[]`. Fine.\n\nNow let me also think about the `InjectionContext` being required. The hard-standard test passes `ctx`. I'll define a ctx in the test: `{ conversationId: 1 }`. Fine.\n\nNow, the `scope` values: \"session\" | \"once\" | \"turn\". The guard uses \"session\". Good.\n\nLet me write injection.ts now. I need to double check: does `lifecycle.ts` importing from `./injection.ts` create a cycle? injection.ts imports from `./injection-guard.ts` only. lifecycle.ts imports injection.ts and injection-guard.ts. No cycle. index.ts imports injection.ts (direct). Good.\n\nOne concern: `injectionGuardEnabled()` reads `process.env.AGENT_INJECTION_GUARD` at call time. In the http probe server (faux), the guard is on by default, so the lifecycle section renders INJECTION_GUARD (same as before). Byte-identical. Good — the probe's system prompt assertions (e.g., `insideSystem.includes(\"当前项目：...\")`) won't be affected. Actually wait — does adding/removing the guard affect the probe? The guard was already there before my change; I'm keeping it identical. So no change. Good.\n\nLet me write injection.ts.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the dedup filter using `.some` — O(n²) but n is tiny (1 provider in batch A). Fine. But to be clean, I could use a Map-based \"last index\" approach. Let me use a cleaner implementation:\n\n```\n// dedupe by id, keep last occurrence in sorted order (后者覆盖)\nconst lastIndexById = new Map<string, number>();\nall.forEach((entry, index) => lastIndexById.set(entry.id, index));\nconst deduped = all.filter((entry, index) => lastIndexById.get(entry.id) === index);\n```\n\nThis keeps each id's last occurrence at its sorted position, preserving order. Cleaner than nested .some. \n\nNow the sort with tiebreakers. Let me reconsider whether including `text`/`channel`/`scope` in the sort could reorder entries in a way that breaks \"按 order 升序（同 order 按 id 字典序）\". The spec says sort by order, then id. My additional tiebreakers only apply when BOTH order and id are equal — i.e., genuinely duplicate ids. In that case, the relative order among those duplicates is otherwise unspecified. Adding deterministic tiebreakers is fine and satisfies \"排序稳定（打乱输入顺序输出一致）\". Good. I'll keep them but comment clearly.\n\nActually, hold on — is there a risk that a reviewer's \"排序稳定\" test provides entries with the SAME order but DIFFERENT ids, and expects them sorted by id? My sort: order (equal), then id → sorted by id. ✓. Good.\n\nAnd \"同 order 按 id 字典序\" — my `cmp(a.id, b.id)` uses `<`/`>` string comparison (lexicographic by UTF-16 code units), which is \"字典序\". ✓.\n\nLet me write the file now."
