ut n is tiny (1 provider in batch A). Fine. But to be clean, I could use a Map-based \"last index\" approach. Let me use a cleaner implementation:\n\n```\n// dedupe by id, keep last occurrence in sorted order (后者覆盖)\nconst lastIndexById = new Map<string, number>();\nall.forEach((entry, index) => lastIndexById.set(entry.id, index));\nconst deduped = all.filter((entry, index) => lastIndexById.get(entry.id) === index);\n```\n\nThis keeps each id's last occurrence at its sorted position, preserving order. Cleaner than nested .some. \n\nNow the sort with tiebreakers. Let me reconsider whether including `text`/`channel`/`scope` in the sort could reorder entries in a way that breaks \"按 order 升序（同 order 按 id 字典序）\". The spec says sort by order, then id. My additional tiebreakers only apply when BOTH order and id are equal — i.e., genuinely duplicate ids. In that case, the relative order among those duplicates is otherwise unspecified. Adding deterministic tiebreakers is fine and satisfies \"排序稳定（打乱输入顺序输出一致）\". Good. I'll keep them but comment clearly.\n\nActually, hold on — is there a risk that a reviewer's \"排序稳定\" test provides entries with the SAME order but DIFFERENT ids, and expects them sorted by id? My sort: order (equal), then id → sorted by id. ✓. Good.\n\nAnd \"同 order 按 id 字典序\" — my `cmp(a.id, b.id)` uses `<`/`>` string comparison (lexicographic by UTF-16 code units), which is \"字典序\". ✓.\n\nLet me write the file now.",
