        "text": "Now write the unit test file for §二. I'll create `injection.test.ts` in `src/agent/`. But I need to add it to the `test` script in package.json (since the test script uses an explicit list). Let me create the test file and then add it to the list.\n\nLet me write comprehensive tests per the task:\n- 条目形状 (entry shape)\n- 排序稳定（打乱输入顺序输出一致）\n- 同 id 去重（后者覆盖）\n- 只接管了一个\n- 审计不返正文\n- Hard standard: `collectInjections([guardProvider], ctx).system[0].text === INJECTION_GUARD`\n\nLet me write injection.test.ts. I'll place it in `src/agent/` alongside other agent tests. Actually, looking at the test list, some agent tests are at `src/agent/partner-context.test.ts`, `src/agent/recover-note.test.ts`, `src/agent/session-search.test.ts`. And others are at `src/` root (e.g., `src/injection-guard.test.ts`). The injection.ts is in `src/agent/`, so I'll put the test in `src/agent/injection.test.ts` and reference it as `src/agent/injection.test.ts` in package.json. Actually, let me put it at `src/injection.test.ts` (root) to match `injection-guard.test.ts` which is at root even though it imports from `./agent/injection-guard.ts`. Hmm, inconsistent. Let me check where injection-guard.test.ts is: `src/injection-guard.test.ts` (root) but imports `./agent/injection-guard.ts`. And injection.ts is in `src/agent/`. \n\nI'll put the new test at `src/agent/injection.test.ts` (matching the source location) and import `./injection.ts` and `./injection-guard.ts` with relative paths from `src/agent/`. That's cleanest. Let me use that.\n\nActually, let me reconsider — the package.json test list has entries like `src/agent/partner-context.test.ts`, `src/agent/recover-note.test.ts`, `src/agent/session-search.test.ts`. So `src/agent/injection.test.ts` fits the pattern. Good.\n\nNow the test content. Let me design carefully.\n\n```ts\nimport assert from \"node:assert/strict\";\nimport { test } from \"node:test\";\nimport {\n  INJECTION_REGISTRY,\n  auditInjections,\n  collectInjections,\n  guardProvider,\n  injectionAuditEnabled,\n  type InjectionContext,\n  type InjectionEntry,\n  type InjectionProvider,\n} from \"./injection.ts\";\nimport { INJECTION_GUARD } from \"./injection-guard.ts\";\n\nconst ctx: InjectionContext = { conversationId: 1 };\n\nfunction entry(partial: Partial<InjectionEntry> & { id: string; text: string }): InjectionEntry {\n  return { order: 0, scope: \"session\", channel: \"system\", ...partial };\n}\n```\n\nWait, `Partial<InjectionEntry> & { id, text }` — spreading gives all fields. Actually `{ order: 0, scope: \"session\", channel: \"system\", ...partial }` — partial can override. But TS: the result type — `order` is `number`, `scope` is `InjectionScope`, `channel` is `InjectionChannel`, plus id/text from partial. But spread of Partial could make them optional... Actually spreading `Partial` after explicit fields: the result has all required fields (explicit ones) plus whatever partial has. But TS might complain that `id` and `text` could be `string | undefined`. Since `partial` is `Partial<InjectionEntry> & {id: string; text: string}`, id/text are required strings. The spread overwrites... hmm, actually spread order: explicit fields first, then `...partial`. If partial has `order` as optional, `order?: number` could override with `undefined`. That would make the result `order: number | undefined`, not assignable to `InjectionEntry`. Let me avoid this and just construct full entries inline in each test, or use a helper with explicit params.\n\nLet me make a cleaner helper:\n\n```ts\nfunction entry(id: string, text: string, order = 0, extra: Partial<InjectionEntry> = {}): InjectionEntry {\n  return { id, text, order, scope: \"session\", channel: \"system\", ...extra };\n}\n```\n\nHere `extra` is `Partial<InjectionEntry>`; spreading it can still override `id`,`text`,`order`,`scope`,`channel` with optional (undefined) values, making the return type not exactly `InjectionEntry`. Hmm. Actually TS: `{ id: string, text: string, order: number, scope: ..., channel: ... } & Partial<InjectionEntry>` — the spread of a Partial produces a type where those props are `T | undefined`. So the overall inferred type would have e.g. `id: string | undefined`. Not assignable to `InjectionEntry`.\n\nTo avoid, I'll just build entries as object literals directly in tests (they're small). Or cast. Let me just write full literals. For \"排序稳定\" test with a few entries, I'll write them out. That's fine and clearer.\n\nActually, a helper that returns a fully-typed entry is convenient. Let me define:\n\n```ts\nconst mk = (id: string, text: string, order: number): InjectionEntry => ({\n  id, text, order, scope: \"session\", channel: \"system\",\n});\n```\n\nFor channel variety, I'll add a separate helper or just inline a message-channel entry. Let me keep it simple.\n\nLet me write the tests:\n\n**Test 1 — 硬标准 + 条目形状:**\n```ts\ntest(\"硬标准：接管后 injection_guard 正文逐字节等于 INJECTION_GUARD\", () => {\n  const prev = process.env.AGENT_INJECTION_GUARD;\n  delete process.env.AGENT_INJECTION_GUARD; // 缺省开，确保 provider 产出\n  try {\n    const collected = collectInjections([guardProvider], ctx);\n    assert.equal(collected.system.length, 1);\n    assert.equal(collected.system[0].text, INJECTION_GUARD);\n    assert.equal(collected.message.length, 0);\n    // 条目形状：恰好 5 个字段\n    const e = collected.system[0];\n    assert.deepEqual(Object.keys(e).sort(), [\"channel\", \"id\", \"order\", \"scope\", \"text\"]);\n    assert.equal(e.id, \"injection_guard\");\n    assert.equal(e.channel, \"system\");\n    assert.equal(e.scope, \"session\");\n    assert.equal(typeof e.order, \"number\");\n  } finally {\n    if (prev === undefined) delete process.env.AGENT_INJECTION_GUARD;\n    else process.env.AGENT_INJECTION_GUARD = prev;\n  }\n});\n```\n\nHmm, `Object.keys(e).sort()` — the entry object is created by guardProvider with keys in order: id, text, order, scope, channel. `Object.keys` returns insertion order: [\"id\",\"text\",\"order\",\"scope\",\"channel\"]. Sorted → [\"channel\",\"id\",\"order\",\"scope\",\"text\"]. ✓. Good.\n\n**Test 2 — 排序稳定（打乱输入顺序输出一致）:**\n```ts\ntest(\"排序稳定：按 order 升序 / 同 order 按 id 字典序；打乱输入顺序输出一致\", () => {\n  const a: InjectionEntry = { id: \"b\", text: \"B\", order: 2, scope: \"session\", channel: \"system\" };\n  const b: InjectionEntry = { id: \"a\", text: \"A\", order: 1, scope: \"session\", channel: \"system\" };\n  const c: InjectionEntry = { id: \"c\", text: \"C\", order: 1, scope: \"session\", channel: \"system\" };\n  const pA: InjectionProvider = () => a;\n  const pB: InjectionProvider = () => b;\n  const pC: InjectionProvider = () => c;\n  const out1 = collectInjections([pA, pB, pC], ctx);\n  const out2 = collectInjections([pC, pA, pB], ctx); // 打乱\n  assert.deepEqual(out1, out2);\n  // 期望顺序：order 1 的 id=a 在 id=c 前（字典序），然后 order 2 的 b\n  assert.deepEqual(out1.system.map((e) => e.id), [\"a\", \"c\", \"b\"]);\n});\n```\nWait: entries a(order2,id=b), b(order1,id=a), c(order1,id=c). Sorted by order then id: b(order1,id=a) → \"a\"; c(order1,id=c) → \"c\"; a(order2,id=b) → \"b\". So ids [\"a\",\"c\",\"b\"]. ✓. And shuffled input gives same. ✓.\n\nAlso test \"同 order 按 id 字典序\": b(id=a,order1) before c(id=c,order1) → \"a\" < \"c\" ✓.\n\n**Test 3 — 同 id 去重（后者覆盖）:**\n```ts\ntest(\"同 id 去重：后者覆盖（保留规范序里最后一次出现）\", () => {\n  const first: InjectionEntry = { id: \"dup\", text: \"FIRST\", order: 1, scope: \"session\", channel: \"system\" };\n  const later: InjectionEntry = { id: \"dup\", text: \"LATER\", order: 2, scope: \"session\", channel: \"system\" };\n  const provider: InjectionProvider = () => [first, later];\n  const out = collectInjections([provider], ctx);\n  assert.equal(out.system.length, 1);\n  assert.equal(out.system[0].text, \"LATER\");\n  assert.equal(out.message.length, 0);\n});\n```\nSorted: first(order1), later(order2) → last index for \"dup\" = 1 (later). deduped = [later]. ✓. text LATER. ✓.\n\nAlso add a cross-provider dedup case to be thorough? Maybe one provider returning array is enough. Let me also add a case where two providers emit same id — \"后者覆盖\" (later provider wins) via stable-ish. Actually with different orders, higher order wins regardless of provider order. Let me add:\n```ts\n// 不同 provider 同 id：order 大的（规范序里靠后）胜出，与 provider 顺序无关\nconst p1: InjectionProvider = () => ({ id:\"x\", text:\"P1\", order:1, scope:\"session\", channel:\"system\" });\nconst p2: InjectionProvider = () => ({ id:\"x\", text:\"P2\", order:2, scope:\"session\", channel:\"system\" });\nassert.equal(collectInjections([p1,p2], ctx).system[0].text, \"P2\");\nassert.equal(collectInjections([p2,p1], ctx).system[0].text, \"P2\");\n```\nBoth give P2 (order 2 > order 1). ✓ deterministic regardless of provider order. Good — this demonstrates \"打乱输入顺序输出一致\" even with dup ids (when orders differ).\n\n**Test 4 — 只接管了一个:**\n```ts\ntest(\"批 A 只接管了一个注入（injection_guard）\", () => {\n  assert.equal(INJECTION_REGISTRY.length, 1);\n  assert.equal(INJECTION_REGISTRY[0].source, \"injection_guard\");\n  // 审计也只有一行\n  const prev = process.env.AGENT_INJECTION_GUARD;\n  delete process.env.AGENT_INJECTION_GUARD;\n  try {\n    assert.equal(auditInjections(ctx).length, 1);\n  } finally { ... }\n});\n```\n\n**Test 5 — 审计不返正文:**\n```ts\ntest(\"审计只回元数据，不回正文\", () => {\n  const prev = process.env.AGENT_INJECTION_GUARD;\n  delete process.env.AGENT_INJECTION_GUARD;\n  try {\n    const rows = auditInjections(ctx);\n    assert.equal(rows.length, 1);\n    const row = rows[0];\n    assert.deepEqual(Object.keys(row).sort(), [\"id\", \"length\", \"scope\", \"source\"]);\n    assert.equal(row.id, \"injection_guard\");\n    assert.equal(row.source, \"injection_guard\");\n    assert.equal(row.scope, \"session\");\n    assert.equal(row.length, INJECTION_GUARD.length);\n    // 没有 text / content / 正文片段\n    assert.ok(!JSON.stringify(rows).includes(INJECTION_GUARD));\n    assert.ok(!(\"text\" in row));\n  } finally { ... }\n});\n```\n\nAlso test `injectionAuditEnabled` default on + false off:\n```ts\ntest(\"审计开关：默认开，显式 false 关\", () => {\n  assert.equal(injectionAuditEnabled({}), true);\n  assert.equal(injectionAuditEnabled({ AGENT_INJECTION_AUDIT: \"FALSE\" }), false);\n  assert.equal(injectionAuditEnabled({ AGENT_INJECTION_AUDIT: \"0\" }), true);\n});\n```\n\nAlso test guardProvider respects the switch (关时省略整段 → byte-identical to before):\n```ts\ntest(\"guardProvider 关时返回 undefined（整段省略，与改造前一致）\", () => {\n  const prev = process.env.AGENT_INJECTION_GUARD;\n  try {\n    process.env.AGENT_INJECTION_GUARD = \"false\";\n    const collected = collectInjections([guardProvider], ctx);\n    assert.equal(collected.system.length, 0);\n    assert.equal(collected.message.length, 0);\n  } finally { ... }\n});\n```\n\nAlso test channel split (分流):\n```ts\ntest(\"按 channel 分流：system / message 各归各位\", () => {\n  const s: InjectionEntry = { id:\"s\", text:\"S\", order:0, scope:\"session\", channel:\"system\" };\n  const m: InjectionEntry = { id:\"m\", text:\"M\", order:0, scope:\"turn\", channel:\"message\" };\n  const out = collectInjections([() => [m, s]], ctx);\n  assert.deepEqual(out.system.map(e=>e.id), [\"s\"]);\n  assert.deepEqual(out.message.map(e=>e.id), [\"m\"]);\n});\n```\n\nThat's a good suite. Let me also make sure I test \"收集 → undefined 忽略\" implicitly (guardProvider off returns undefined). Covered.\n\nNow, about env manipulation: I'll write a helper to save/restore `AGENT_INJECTION_GUARD`. Actually, multiple tests delete it. Since each test file runs in its own process, and within the file tests run sequentially, I need to be careful: if test A deletes the env and restores in finally, fine. But `delete` then restore. Let me write a helper `withGuardOn(fn)` that ensures guard is on during fn and restores after. Actually simpler: since default is on, and injection-guard.test.ts (separate process) handles the off case, I can just NOT delete in most tests — rely on default on. But to be bulletproof against `AGENT_INJECTION_GUARD` being set in the environment by the developer, I'll add a `before`/`after` hook at the file level:\n\n```ts\nimport { before, after } from \"node:test\";\nlet saved: string | undefined;\nbefore(() => { saved = process.env.AGENT_INJECTION_GUARD; delete process.env.AGENT_INJECTION_GUARD; });\nafter(() => { if (saved === undefined) delete process.env.AGENT_INJECTION_GUARD; else process.env.AGENT_INJECTION_GUARD = saved; });\n```\n\nWait, but the \"guardProvider 关时返回 undefined\" test sets it to \"false\" inside the test and restores via try/finally. If I have a global `before` that deletes it, and the test sets it to false then restores to undefined (delete), that's fine. But the `after` hook restores the original saved value. OK. But there's a subtlety: `before`/`after` in node:test at top level run once for the file. Tests run after `before`. Good.\n\nActually, to keep it simple and avoid hook complexity, I'll just explicitly manage env in each test that needs guard ON by using a helper:\n\n```ts\nfunction guardOn(): () => void {\n  const prev = process.env.AGENT_INJECTION_GUARD;\n  delete process.env.AGENT_INJECTION_GUARD;\n  return () => {\n    if (prev === undefined) delete process.env.AGENT_INJECTION_GUARD;\n    else process.env.AGENT_INJECTION_GUARD = prev;\n  };\n}\n```\n\nHmm, that's a bit awkward. Let me just use module-level `before`/`after` hooks — cleanest. Most tests rely on guard ON (set once by `before`), and the one test that sets it OFF restores it. Let me do that.\n\nActually wait — `before` deletes AGENT_INJECTION_GUARD, making guard ON for all tests. The \"guardProvider off\" test sets it to \"false\" during the test and restores to undefined in finally. Fine. `after` restores the original env. \n\nBut hold on: does the http-probe or other external process set AGENT_INJECTION_GUARD in the test env? No, tests run via `pnpm --filter @lifediary/server test` which doesn't set it. So default is on. The `before` hook is just a safety net. But adding `before`/`after` from `node:test` — is that available? Yes, `node:test` exports `before`, `after`, `beforeEach`, `afterEach`. tsx supports it.\n\nActually, simplest: I'll avoid hooks and just add a single line at module top: none. Let me just rely on default-on but ALSO make the tests that need guard-on robust by not depending on env at all for the HARD STANDARD. \n\nHmm, wait. The hard-standard test MUST have guard ON. If I rely on default and the environment happens to have AGENT_INJECTION_GUARD=false, the test would fail. But that's an unusual environment. To be safe, I'll use the `before` hook. Let me use it.\n\nActually, you know what — let me reconsider making guardProvider NOT env-dependent and instead handle the switch in lifecycle. That would make the hard-standard test bulletproof (always passes). But I already committed to Option A. Let me reconsider the tradeoff once more:\n\nOption A (guardProvider reads env): framework owns everything, cleaner takeover. Hard-standard test needs env on. I'll ensure via `before` hook. Fine.\n\nActually, there's a subtle issue with Option A + the audit endpoint: when `AGENT_INJECTION_GUARD=false`, `auditInjections(ctx)` returns `[]` (guardProvider returns undefined). That's correct behavior (no injection active). Good.\n\nOK, let me just use the `before` hook. Let me write the test file.\n\nActually, let me simplify: I'll not use `before`/`after`; instead, in EACH test that needs guard on, I'll call a tiny helper. But that's repetitive. The `before` hook is cleaner. Let me use it. Actually, even simpler and very common in this repo (see injection-guard.test.ts): they do `const previous = process.env.X; try { ... } finally { restore }` inside each test. Let me follow that repo pattern for consistency, but it's verbose for 5 tests.\n\nCompromise: use top-level `before`/`after` hooks (node:test supports them). Clean. Let me verify tsx/node version supports. Node 20+ (package uses @types/node ^22). Yes.\n\nLet me write the test file now.\n\nOne more: for \"只接管了一个\" test, I assert `INJECTION_REGISTRY.length === 1`. Good.\n\nLet me also include the hard-standard assertion EXACTLY as the task stated: `collectInjections([guardProvider], ctx).system[0].text === INJECTION_GUARD`. Yes, I have that.\n\nLet me write it."
      }
    ],
