      {
        "type": "reasoning_text",
        "text": "All 9 injection tests pass. Now I need to verify the \"逐字节一致\" hard standard more rigorously. The task says \"接管后 injection_guard 的注入内容与位置必须与改造前逐字节一致\". The unit test asserts collectInjections text === INJECTION_GUARD. But I should also verify:\n1. The section is still at the same index in the sections array (position).\n2. The rendered text is byte-identical.\n\nI've kept the section at index 3 (unchanged) and render now returns `collectInjections([guardProvider], ctx).system[0]?.text` = INJECTION_GUARD. That's byte-identical. I'm confident. Let me also double-check there are no OTHER changes to lifecycle that could affect it (I only changed the import + the one section). Let me grep to confirm the section key and that INJECTION_GUARD isn't referenced directly anymore (it's now via framework).\n\nAlso, I should consider adding an http-probe check to actually exercise the `/injections` endpoint end-to-end. The task requires `pnpm --filter @lifediary/server http` to pass (which it will regardless), but verifying the endpoint actually works via HTTP would be good. However, adding to the http-probe.ts is optional. Let me think about whether it's worth it.\n\nActually, the task §2.3 defines an audit endpoint. A thorough implementation would verify it works over real HTTP. The task says \"每完成一节跑一次 typecheck + test\" and \"最后跑 http\". It doesn't explicitly require adding a probe assertion. But \"审计端点\" is a deliverable; verifying it via the http probe is the \"探针都是真起服务 + 真 HTTP\" principle. Let me add a lightweight check to the http-probe to confirm the endpoint. But I need to be careful: the http probe restarts the server many times with different envs. I'll add the check early (in section 4 area, where `externalId` is available and the server is the initial faux one). \n\nActually, where's a good spot? After section 1 creates `externalId`, section 2-4 use it. I could add a check right after section 4 (用量) or as a new \"§4.5\". Let me add a small block after section 3 (or 4) that GETs `/injections` and verifies:\n- status 200\n- returns an array with 1 row: `{id:\"injection_guard\", source:\"injection_guard\", scope:\"session\", length:>0}`\n- no `text` field, no `INJECTION_GUARD` content.\n\nBut wait — the guard content `INJECTION_GUARD` contains Chinese text. I'd need to check the response doesn't contain it. But I can't easily import INJECTION_GUARD into the probe... actually I can: `import { INJECTION_GUARD } from \"../src/agent/injection-guard.ts\"`. But that adds a dependency. Alternatively, just check the row keys don't include \"text\" and length is a number. That's sufficient for \"不返正文\".\n\nAlso I should test the rollback switch `AGENT_INJECTION_AUDIT=false` → 404. That requires restarting the server with that env, which is more involved. Hmm. Let me keep the probe check minimal: just verify 200 + shape on the initial server. Actually, testing the 404 rollback would be nice but adds a server restart. \n\nHmm, let me reconsider — is it worth modifying the big http-probe.ts? Risk: introducing a bug in the probe, or the check failing due to env. Let me think about what the endpoint returns in the probe's faux environment:\n- `injectionAuditEnabled()` → default true (env doesn't set AGENT_INJECTION_AUDIT). ✓ 200.\n- `auditInjections(ctx)` → guardProvider checks `injectionGuardEnabled()` → default on (env doesn't set AGENT_INJECTION_GUARD). ✓ 1 row.\n- Row: `{id:\"injection_guard\", source:\"injection_guard\", scope:\"session\", length: INJECTION_GUARD.length}`. ✓.\n\nSo a check would pass. Let me add it. It genuinely verifies the endpoint over real HTTP, which aligns with the repo's \"真 HTTP 探针\" principle and the task's requirement that the http probe passes.\n\nActually, I realize I should be careful: adding a check that could FAIL would make `pnpm http` fail. But I've reasoned it passes. Let me add it but keep it robust.\n\nWhere to insert: Let me add a new section right after section 4 (用量), before section 5 (提醒). Actually, let me add it as section \"4.5\" after the usage check. Let me find the exact anchor: after line 382-383 (usage check) and before \"// ── 5. 提醒\". Let me re-read that area.\n\nLines 376-384:\n```\n\t// ── 4. 用量 ─────────────────────────────────────────\n\tconsole.log(\"\\n· 4. 用量接口\");\n\tconst usageResponse = await fetch(`${BASE}/api/v1/agent/usage`);\n\tconst usage = (await usageResponse.json()) as {\n\t\tmodels: { model: string; totalTokens: number }[];\n\t};\n\tcheck(usage.models.length > 0, \"有用量记录\", JSON.stringify(usage.models[0]));\n\n\t// ── 5. 提醒 ─────────────────────────────────────────\n```\n\nI'll insert between them. Let me write the check. Actually, to test the 404 rollback too, I'd need a server restart. Let me NOT restart — just do the positive check on the current server. Add a note that the rollback switch is covered by unit test. Actually, I could also do a quick check with a SECOND server. But that complicates. Let me keep it to the positive check plus maybe verify \"不返正文\" by checking keys.\n\nHmm, actually, wait — there's a subtlety. The http probe uses `externalId` from section 1. But actually I realize the audit endpoint doesn't need a valid conversation, so I can use any id. I'll use `externalId` for realism.\n\nLet me write:\n```ts\n\t// ── 4.5 注入审计（2026-11-10 批 26b §二）──────────\n\tconsole.log(\"\\n· 4.5 注入审计端点（只回元数据，不回正文）\");\n\tconst injectionsRes = await fetch(`${BASE}/api/v1/agent/chat/${externalId}/injections`);\n\tcheck(injectionsRes.status === 200, \"GET .../injections → 200\", String(injectionsRes.status));\n\tconst injections = (await injectionsRes.json()) as Array<Record<string, unknown>>;\n\tcheck(Array.isArray(injections) && injections.length === 1, \"批 A 只接管一个注入\", JSON.stringify(injections));\n\tconst injRow = injections[0] ?? {};\n\tcheck(\n\t\tinjRow.id === \"injection_guard\" &&\n\t\t\tinjRow.source === \"injection_guard\" &&\n\t\t\tinjRow.scope === \"session\" &&\n\t\t\ttypeof injRow.length === \"number\" &&\n\t\t\t(injRow.length as number) > 0,\n\t\t\"审计行形状 {id,source,scope,length}\",\n\t\tJSON.stringify(injRow),\n\t);\n\tcheck(!(\"text\" in injRow) && !JSON.stringify(injections).includes(\"【材料安全】\"), \"审计不返正文\");\n```\n\nWait, \"【材料安全】\" is the first line of INJECTION_GUARD. Checking the response doesn't include it verifies no正文. Good, no need to import. Actually let me double check INJECTION_GUARD starts with \"【材料安全】\" — yes, line 12 of injection-guard.ts: `\"【材料安全】\",`. Good.\n\nThis is a nice end-to-end verification. Let me add it. But hold on — will this break if `AGENT_INJECTION_GUARD` is somehow set in the probe env? The probe's `startServer` spreads `...process.env` then sets specific vars. It doesn't set AGENT_INJECTION_GUARD, so if the parent process has it set to false, the probe would inherit it. But that's unlikely, and actually the gate (deploy) env wouldn't set it. Also `AGENT_INJECTION_AUDIT` — not set. Original `process.env` from the runner. To be safe, I could add `AGENT_INJECTION_GUARD: \"on\"` and `AGENT_INJECTION_AUDIT: \"\"` to the probe's startServer env. Actually, adding these to `startServer`'s env would make it deterministic across ALL server restarts. But that changes every server instance. Hmm. Actually, since the guard was ALREADY being injected before my change (the section existed), the probe already relies on guard being on by default. So no new risk. But to be safe for the new audit check, let me not over-engineer — the default is on.\n\nActually, wait — there's a real consideration: the audit switch `AGENT_INJECTION_AUDIT` default is on. If the developer's shell has `AGENT_INJECTION_AUDIT=false` exported, the probe would get 404 and my check fails. But that's an unusual env. To be robust, I'll add `AGENT_INJECTION_AUDIT: \"\"` (force default on) to the probe's base env in `startServer`. Hmm, but that's a change to the shared env object. Actually it's harmless and makes it deterministic. But it's a bit of an over-reach. Let me think...\n\nActually, I'll keep it simple: add the positive check but make it tolerant — no, a check that expects 200 should be right. Let me just add the env override in startServer to be safe. Actually, the cleanest: since the probe already spreads `...process.env` and explicitly clears several vars (e.g., `CURRENT_PROJECT: \"\"`, `AUTONOMY_ENABLED: \"\"`), I'll add `AGENT_INJECTION_AUDIT: \"\"` and `AGENT_INJECTION_GUARD: \"\"` to that clear list. Wait — but clearing `AGENT_INJECTION_GUARD` to \"\" means \"on\" (default), which matches the pre-existing behavior. Good. And clearing `AGENT_INJECTION_AUDIT` to \"\" means on. This makes the probe deterministic. \n\nHmm, but actually these two env vars weren't cleared before, meaning the probe previously inherited them if set. My guard section code change doesn't alter that (guardProvider still reads AGENT_INJECTION_GUARD). So behavior is consistent. Adding the clears is a minor robustness improvement. Let me do it to be safe — actually, no. Let me minimize changes to the probe. I'll just add the check block and rely on defaults. If `pnpm http` passes, great. If not, I'll investigate. Let me add the check and run http.\n\nActually, I realize I should be more careful: let me just add the check block without touching startServer env. Run http. If it passes, done. Let me do that.\n\nWait, actually — do I even want to add the http probe check? Let me reconsider the risk/reward once more:\n- Reward: real end-to-end verification of the audit endpoint (aligns with repo principle, strengthens \"验了什么\").\n- Risk: low, as reasoned.\n\nYes, worth it. Let me add it.\n\nActually, hold on. Let me reconsider — the task says \"审计端点参考 index.ts 里其它 ... 的写法\". And validation requires `pnpm --filter @lifediary/server http` to pass. Since the probe is the \"http\" validation, and my endpoint is new, adding a check there is the most honest way to claim \"验了 http 端点\". I'll do it.\n\nLet me insert the check."
      }
